{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-49570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n+3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-49570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{n+3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{n}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{x}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{x}{3}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<x\\le 3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-49570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "841b1408198f9c3de27018c6f35978cee25e8a1928ef25cc6dab4f9a30eebe2e"} {"question" "q-49570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-49570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<x\\le 3"] ["math" "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "x>0"] ["math" "x\\ge 3"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-49570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0<x\\le 3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-123440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-123440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {x}^{2n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =x\\cdot {x}^{2n-n} \\\\  & =x\\cdot {x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-123440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "841b1408198f9c3de27018c6f35978cee25e8a1928ef25cc6dab4f9a30eebe2e"} {"question" "q-123440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-123440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<x\\le 1"] ["math" "0<x\\le 2"] ["math" "x>0"] ["math" "x\\ge 1"] ["math" "x\\ge 2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-123440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0<x\\le 1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=8{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThis given sequence is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges if \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "841b1408198f9c3de27018c6f35978cee25e8a1928ef25cc6dab4f9a30eebe2e"} {"question" "q-141322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<x\\le 2"] ["math" "0<x\\le 4"] ["math" "x>0"] ["math" "x\\ge 2"] ["math" "x\\ge 4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0<x\\le 2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=2x{(\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}.$ \nSince ${x}^{4}$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{4}\\ge 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "841b1408198f9c3de27018c6f35978cee25e8a1928ef25cc6dab4f9a30eebe2e"} {"question" "q-141329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<x\\le 4"] ["math" "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "x\\ge 2"] ["math" "x\\ge 4"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-141329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge 2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-347770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n$x$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-347770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{5}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot {5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{5}{x})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{5}{x}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{5}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041740/state.json" "file_sha256" "e78e47b1d473b099fb5d7b0123d7336cfe4dfd91860810ceb20ccb940e6cb891"} {"question" "q-347770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/questio